TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"2.2 Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Appendix A: STK files and settings"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Contents"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"The stated orbital-decay values are approximately ten times too large for the given equation and inputs. At 600 km, Δa per orbit is about 0.040 m for 0.3 m² and 0.57 m for 4.3 m², not 0.40 m and 5.8 m. Corresponding initial decay rates are about 0.22 km/year and 3.1 km/year; the H/rate estimates are about 320 years and 23 years, respectively, not 32 and 2.2 years. Thus the report does not establish that a 4 m² sail meets the 25-year guideline."},{"where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"The claim of about 11 km altitude loss over five years follows the same factor-of-ten error; the stated inputs imply about 1.1 km using the report's approximation."},{"where":"2.3 Orbit","issue":"The five-day access estimate divides equatorial ground-track spacing by the maximum roll-reachable width. It is a simplified upper-bound argument and does not demonstrate actual reef-specific revisit or imaging opportunities, which depend on ground-track geometry, pointing, lighting, and cloud conditions."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","issue":"A 50 Mbit/s downlink is assumed without a link budget. The report acknowledges this gap, so the stated capacity margin is not yet verified against transmitter power, antenna gains, range, coding, and losses."}],"overall":"A clear, well-supported preliminary mission design with strong mission rationale, useful STK evidence, and detailed orbit and communications work. The orbital-decay arithmetic error materially invalidates the claimed drag-sail lifetime and EOL compliance; the link budget and revisit analysis also need validation. Recalculate disposal performance and verify communications and coverage before treating the design as feasible.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The mission, Australian benefit, rationale, single-spacecraft choice, operator, customers, and public-service funding are clearly explained. References support the rationale, although some sources are secondary or incompletely detailed.","evidence":[{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The primary mission of ZYZZsat is to image the whole Great Barrier Reef in visible bands at 5 m resolution"},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"One satellite is therefore used."},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The operator is the Australian Institute of Marine Science (AIMS)"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":36.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report addresses launch, orbit and elements, access calculations, station, communications, duration, and disposal, with STK figures and detailed calculations. However, the orbital-decay calculation is wrong by about a factor of ten, undermining its EOL compliance claim; a link budget is also absent, and the orbit/revisit and payload assumptions are preliminary.","evidence":[{"where":"2.2 Launch","quote":"The baseline is an SSMS rideshare flight on Vega C from Kourou in early 2025"},{"where":"2.3 Orbit","quote":"The orbit is circular, so it has no perigee"},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","quote":"There are six passes each day, three in the evening and three in the morning (UTC)"},{"where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"With a 4 m² sail the satellite re-enters about two years after the end of the mission."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion synthesizes key design results and identifies meaningful limitations and follow-up work. It is specific to the evidence, though its positive EOL conclusion relies on the erroneous decay calculation.","evidence":[{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"The design has four limitations."},{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"With these points addressed in the next design phase, the mission can provide the direct, regular bleaching maps"}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a logical structure, concise executive summary, labelled figures and tables, readable technical prose, and a substantial bibliography with in-text citations. Presentation is generally professional. Some sources are weakly specified and some figures are not accompanied by underlying software outputs in the report, but the appendix identifies submitted STK files.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report presents the design of ZYZZsat (Z-axis/Y-axis Zeroed Zoning Satellite)"},{"where":"Contents","quote":"Executive Summary
1 Introduction
2 Mission Design
2.1 Spacecraft overview"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[11] D. A. Vallado, *Fundamentals of Astrodynamics and Applications*"},{"where":"Image 1","quote":""}]}]}
